# Weekly Client Documentation System

## Auto-Documentation Rule
Automatically update `WEEKLY-CLIENT-UPDATES.md` when completing work that meets ANY of these criteria:

### Documentation Triggers (Smart Detection)
1. **Feature Deployment**: Any new user-facing feature that goes to production
2. **Critical Fix**: Bug fix that affected user experience, authentication, data integrity, or system stability
3. **Integration**: New third-party service, API, or platform connection established
4. **Infrastructure**: Database migrations, hosting changes, deployment configuration, or architecture updates
5. **Performance**: Measurable improvements (>20% faster load times, reduced costs, improved efficiency)
6. **UX Overhaul**: Significant design changes, workflow improvements, or accessibility enhancements

### When to Update (Automatic)
- ✅ After successful deployment to production/staging
- ✅ After completing multi-file feature implementation spanning >3 files
- ✅ After fixing a bug that required >30 minutes of work
- ✅ After integration testing passes for new services
- ✅ After database schema changes are migrated
- ✅ After completing a task that directly impacts client's business objectives

### When NOT to Update (Skip These)
- ❌ Minor CSS adjustments, text changes, or styling tweaks
- ❌ Exploratory work, code reviews, or investigative debugging
- ❌ Failed attempts or reverted changes
- ❌ Routine maintenance, dependency updates, or housekeeping
- ❌ Chat discussions, planning sessions, or brainstorming
- ❌ Single-file changes under 50 lines (unless critical)

### Entry Format (Use This Template)
When updating `WEEKLY-CLIENT-UPDATES.md`, add entries in this format:

```markdown
### [Client-Friendly Feature Title]
- **Completed**: YYYY-MM-DD
- **Category**: Feature | Bug Fix | Integration | Performance | Infrastructure | UX
- **Client Impact**: [ONE sentence explaining business value - what this means for their customers/revenue/efficiency]
- **Details**: [2-3 sentences max - what was built/fixed, key technologies used, measurable outcomes]
- **Status**: ✅ Deployed | 🧪 Testing | 📋 Staged for Review
```

### Client-Friendly Language Guidelines
- Use **business outcomes** over technical jargon
  - ✅ "Customers can now save projects and return later"
  - ❌ "Implemented LocalStorage persistence layer"
- Use **plain English** for features
  - ✅ "Payment processing now accepts credit cards"
  - ❌ "Integrated Stripe SDK with webhook handlers"
- Focus on **impact metrics** when available
  - ✅ "Reduced page load time by 45% (3.2s → 1.8s)"
  - ❌ "Optimized render performance"
- Use **customer-centric terms**
  - ✅ "Dashboard", "Customers", "Faster experience"
  - ❌ "Admin panel", "Users", "Reduced latency"

### Update Process
1. When completing qualifying work, immediately append to "Current Week" section
2. Use the exact template format above
3. Keep entries concise (under 100 words total)
4. Focus on **why it matters** to the client, not how it was built
5. If uncertain whether to log, err on the side of logging (can be removed later)
6. At start of new week, move previous week's entries to "Previous Updates"

### Example of Good Entry
```
### AI-Powered Analytics Dashboard
- **Completed**: 2025-01-09
- **Category**: Feature
- **Client Impact**: Business owners can now track AI project ROI with real-time cost analysis and performance metrics
- **Details**: Built interactive dashboard showing project status, AI model usage patterns, and cost breakdowns. Integrated with existing project management system. Reduces reporting time from hours to seconds.
- **Status**: ✅ Deployed
```

### Example of Entry That Should NOT Be Logged
```
### Fixed Button Padding Issue
- Minor CSS adjustment to navigation button spacing
- Changed padding from 10px to 12px for better alignment
- Single-file change, purely aesthetic
```




